  "narrative_llm": "<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Short impact summary</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Revival After the Great War is showing an early but clear pattern of scholarly and public use. The book has 4 OpenAlex citations, 3,477 recorded downloads, and visible discussion on Wikipedia, indicating that it is being read and noticed beyond its immediate publication context. Its reach also extends into work on reconstruction, heritage, and post-conflict recovery, with citations appearing in history, architecture, and civil engineering venues.</p>\n<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Full narrative</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Revival After the Great War, published by Leuven University Press in 2021, examines post-First World War recovery across social, political, and architectural dimensions. The impact record suggests a work that has begun to circulate across both scholarly and public-facing channels. Its 4 citations are an early signal of academic uptake rather than a mature citation profile, but the citing landscape is notable for its breadth, reaching into history, architecture, civil engineering, and related areas concerned with memory, heritage, and reconstruction.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Usage data show stronger evidence of readership than citations alone might imply. The book has been downloaded 3,477 times through OAPEN, which points to sustained access and consultation. Open access availability likely supports that reach: the record identifies the book as openly available, with multiple open instances indexed in repository and discovery systems. That combination of open access and download activity suggests the book is serving as a usable reference point for readers working on postwar rebuilding and its longer-term social consequences.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Public visibility is also present in knowledge-base and community contexts. The record shows Wikipedia activity, indicating that the book\u2019s themes or related material have entered public reference spaces. That kind of mention does not equal scholarly citation, but it does show that the work is participating in broader public discourse around war memory, recovery, and the built environment. The thematic alignment with Sustainable Cities and Communities further reinforces the book\u2019s policy-adjacent relevance, especially where historical reconstruction informs present-day thinking about damaged places and urban renewal.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Overall, the evidence points to a book with growing interdisciplinary resonance and clear signs of readership, while the citation base remains modest and still developing. The strongest story here is one of access and circulation: the book is being downloaded, discussed in public knowledge spaces, and cited by later work that spans humanities and design-oriented fields. That pattern is consistent with a publication that is beginning to shape conversations about post-war restoration and the reconstruction of societies and cities after conflict.</p>",
